 "Platform clock in poor condition. Please report to Station Master's office for maintenance instructions. All other clocks in use."
It is likely that the platform clock, located where commuters wait for trains, was in disrepair and needed maintenance to ensure it continued to function correctly. The station master's office is responsible for managing the daily operations of the train station, including the maintenance of equipment like clocks. The phrase "all other clocks in use" suggests that there were other clocks in the station that still functioned properly. This bulletin aims to inform commuters and station employees of the issue and provide guidance on how to address it. It is a standard procedure for train stations to have such a system in place for reporting and addressing equipment issues to ensure a smooth and efficient operation. (Source:  British Railways) 1960-1970. (Color photograph)
Title: Maintaining a Platform Clock
Keywords: British Railways, Platform clock, Maintenance, Station Master's office, Commuters, Train station, Operations
Date: 1960-1970
Description: A station bulletin about maintaining a platform clock from the 1960s. It warns of a clock in poor condition and instructs the public to report any issues to the Station Master's office. The notice also mentions that other clocks in use are still functioning properly. This is a standard procedure for train stations to follow in order to ensure the smooth operation of the station. (Source:  British Railways)
